  "ai_show_review": {
    "summary": "High‑energy Nassau ’82 with scorching Peggy‑O, Sugaree and a lyrical Estimated Prophet.",
    "review": "April 12 1982 at the Nassau Coliseum is one of those early‑’80s shows that really captures the Dead in top form. The band launches into a raucous first set – Iko Iko and New Minglewood Blues set the tone, then Jerry’s floating lead on Peggy‑O and a tight, punchy Take Jed keep the groove rolling through Deal. The second set is where the magic peaks: a rollicking Man Smart → Sugaree segues into an almost lyrical Estimated Prophet, followed by a soaring Uncle John’s Band that melts straight into a classic The Other One → Stella Blue → Sugar Magnolia suite. The encore’s Satisfaction lands with the kind of crowd‑sing energy you only get in a packed New York arena. Across all recordings the performance feels locked in, especially on the standout tracks Peggy‑O, Sugaree and Estimated Prophet, while the rhythm section drives an unrelenting high‑energy flow from start to finish.",
